      "title": "Evaluating the Derivative at a Point Using the Chain Rule"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "5da76883-22fb-51f9-92d9-33f75d3543ee",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]$ at $t=2$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\left(6 x\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(4 y\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\left(e^{- 2 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(e^{3 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\left(6 x\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(4 y\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=e^{- 2 t}$ and $y=e^{3 t}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\left(6 x\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(4 y\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}\\Big|_{x=e^{- 2 t},\\,y=e^{3 t}} \\\\  & =\\left(6 e^{- 2 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(4 e^{3 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(e^{- 2 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(e^{3 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\left(- 2 e^{- 2 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(3 e^{3 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(6 e^{- 2 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(4 e^{3 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(- 2 e^{- 2 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(3 e^{3 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(6 e^{- 2 t}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 2 e^{- 2 t}\\right)+\\left(4 e^{3 t}\\right)\\cdot \\left(3 e^{3 t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(- 12 e^{- 4 t}\\right)+\\left(12 e^{6 t}\\right) \\\\  & =12 \\left(e^{t} - 1\\right) \\left(e^{t} + 1\\right) \\left(e^{4 t} - e^{3 t} + e^{2 t} - e^{t} + 1\\right) \\left(e^{4 t} + e^{3 t} + e^{2 t} + e^{t} + 1\\right) e^{- 4 t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]\\Big|_{t=2} & =12 \\left(e^{t} - 1\\right) \\left(e^{t} + 1\\right) \\left(e^{4 t} - e^{3 t} + e^{2 t} - e^{t} + 1\\right) \\left(e^{4 t} + e^{3 t} + e^{2 t} + e^{t} + 1\\right) e^{- 4 t}\\Big|_{t=2} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{12}{e^{8}}\\right)+\\left(12 e^{12}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{12 \\left(-1 + e\\right) \\left(1 + e\\right) \\left(1 + e^{2}\\right) \\left(1 + e + e^{2} + e^{3} + e^{4}\\right) \\left(- e^{3} - e + 1 + e^{2} + e^{4}\\right) \\left(- e^{6} - e^{2} + 1 + e^{4} + e^{8}\\right)}{e^{8}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "5af915cb-4e7b-5c6f-a90e-fdff5310d8ec",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
