    "question": {
      ":question/id": "77e30e11-f999-562a-8163-aa5a9f75649f",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]$ at $t=1$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\left(\\frac{6 x}{3 x^{2} + 4 y}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(\\frac{4}{3 x^{2} + 4 y}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\left(t\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(4 t^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\left(\\frac{6 x}{3 x^{2} + 4 y}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(\\frac{4}{3 x^{2} + 4 y}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=t$ and $y=4 t^{2}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\left(\\frac{6 x}{3 x^{2} + 4 y}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(\\frac{4}{3 x^{2} + 4 y}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}\\Big|_{x=t,\\,y=4 t^{2}} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{6}{19 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(\\frac{4}{19 t^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(t\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(4 t^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\left(1\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(8 t\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\frac{6}{19 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(\\frac{4}{19 t^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(1\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(8 t\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{6}{19 t}\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right)+\\left(\\frac{4}{19 t^{2}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(8 t\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{6}{19 t}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{32}{19 t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]\\Big|_{t=1} & =\\frac{2}{t}\\Big|_{t=1} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{6}{19}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{32}{19}\\right) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
