    "question": {
      ":question/id": "820f1d95-ec39-55aa-96a2-a1cb8171d952",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]$ at $t=2$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\left(6 x\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(2 y\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\left(e^{- 3 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(e^{2 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\left(6 x\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(2 y\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=e^{- 3 t}$ and $y=e^{2 t}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\left(6 x\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(2 y\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}\\Big|_{x=e^{- 3 t},\\,y=e^{2 t}} \\\\  & =\\left(6 e^{- 3 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(2 e^{2 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(e^{- 3 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(e^{2 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\left(- 3 e^{- 3 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(2 e^{2 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(6 e^{- 3 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(2 e^{2 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\left(- 3 e^{- 3 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\left(2 e^{2 t}\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(6 e^{- 3 t}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 3 e^{- 3 t}\\right)+\\left(2 e^{2 t}\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 e^{2 t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(- 18 e^{- 6 t}\\right)+\\left(4 e^{4 t}\\right) \\\\  & =2 \\left(2 e^{10 t} - 9\\right) e^{- 6 t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=2,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]\\Big|_{t=2} & =2 \\left(2 e^{10 t} - 9\\right) e^{- 6 t}\\Big|_{t=2} \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{18}{e^{12}}\\right)+\\left(4 e^{8}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\left(-9 + 2 e^{20}\\right)}{e^{12}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
